---
import SeoLayout from '../../layouts/SeoLayout.astro';
import { personaBySlug, isPublished, personaUrl, guideUrl } from '../../lib/seo-routes';
import { APP_URL } from '../../lib/site';

const persona = personaBySlug('procurement');
const title = 'Live translation for procurement and supplier calls';
const description =
  'Talk to the production manager, not the sales contact who happens to speak English. ' +
  'Per-room glossaries for commercial terms, published pricing, credits that never expire.';
---

<SeoLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={personaUrl(persona.slug)}
  noindex={!isPublished(persona)}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      The problem in supplier calls is rarely translation quality. It is that everything routes
      through whoever on the supplier's side speaks your language best — usually a commercial
      contact rather than the person who knows the answer.
    </p>

    <h2>What changes when you can talk to the operational people</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Quality issues get discussed with the person who runs the process</li>
      <li>Delivery dates come from the production planner, not from a relayed assurance</li>
      <li>A factory audit is answered by the floor, not narrated by sales</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That is the actual value of translation here. It is not about comfort — it changes
      <strong>who can be in the conversation</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where commercial vocabulary breaks</h2>
    <p>
      General translation handles ordinary speech well and business terms badly, because the
      business sense diverges from the everyday sense of the same word: <em>purchase order</em>,
      <em>lead time</em>, <em>backorder</em>, <em>minimum order quantity</em>, <em>net thirty</em>,
      <em>incoterms</em>. A wrong rendering here is expensive rather than embarrassing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Per-room glossaries let you fix your terms once and reuse them on every call with that
      supplier.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the native tooling usually does not fit</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Teams Interpreter excludes external guests and anonymous users</strong>, and needs
        Microsoft 365 Copilot. Suppliers are external by definition.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Meet supports one English-anchored language pair per meeting</strong> — so a call
        with a Chinese-speaking production team and your Spanish-speaking quality lead is outside it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zoom gives captions only</strong>, awkward for someone walking a factory floor.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When you should not choose us</h2>
    <p>
      For contract negotiation — terms, liability, penalties — hire a human interpreter. The
      judgement matters more than the words, and we will tell you so. We are for the fortnightly
      production call, the quality escalation and the audit, which currently happen in approximate
      English or not at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('negotiate-with-a-supplier-who-doesnt-speak-english')}
          >Negotiating with a supplier who doesn't speak English</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('factory-audit-call-language-barrier')}
          >Running a factory audit call across a language barrier</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('supplier-qualification-call-translation')}
          >Translating a supplier qualification call</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta">
      <a href={APP_URL}>Try it on a real supplier call</a> — no contract, no minimum.
    </p>
  </article>
</SeoLayout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  li {
    margin-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cta {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
